C = [
("h1", "8. Hebrews 10: The Same Verdict from the Other Side"),
("p", "Claim: the letter to the Hebrews takes up the sentence of Psalm 40 and says, in its own argument, what the prophets had been saying about the offerings: they were never the means by which sins are taken away. It does not say the prophets abolished them. It says that the offerings of the Law were a shadow and could not take away sins, that the psalm sets the will done against them, and that Christ’s offering of his body once for all does what they could not. The letter is read inside Israel’s line, on its own audience markers and the covenant it quotes, and builds nothing for the Body from it."),
("sub", "8.1 Where the letter stands"),
("p", "This reading rests on the letter’s own audience markers and on the covenant it quotes (Jeremiah 31:31–34), which belongs to the house of Israel and the house of Judah, the Body benefiting from Christ’s blood without being the covenant party. The letter speaks of the priests who “offer gifts according to the law” (Hebrews 8:4), of Moses and the tabernacle (Hebrews 8:5), and of a new covenant made with Israel (Hebrews 8:8–12 quotes it). It is addressed to men who know the sacrifices and have a high priest in view. Study 24 reads the letter’s warning and its sacrificial argument, and Typology Commentary Volumes 1 and 3 read the shadow and the priesthood. This chapter asks one narrower question: how does the letter take up the psalm’s sentence on the offerings, and what does it make of it?"),
("sub", "8.2 The Law’s sacrifices: a shadow that could not take away sins"),
("p", "The tenth chapter begins with a verdict on the Law’s offerings. “For the law having a shadow of good things to come, and not the very image of the things, can never with those sacrifices which they offered year by year continually make the comers thereunto perfect. For then would they not have ceased to be offered? because that the worshippers once purged should have had no more conscience of sins. But in those sacrifices there is a remembrance again made of sins every year. For it is not possible that the blood of bulls and of goats should take away sins” (Hebrews 10:1–4)."),
("p", "The sentence is flat, and it does not say the sacrifices were wrong. It says what they could not do. The repetition of the sacrifices, year by year, is the proof: a thing that took sin away would not need to be repeated. The writer’s argument is that the Law’s sacrifices were a shadow and a yearly remembrance of sins. This is a statement about their power, and it is made inside the covenant the sacrifices belonged to. The same writer says elsewhere that the Law’s sacrifices were a figure “imposed on them until the time of reformation” (Hebrews 9:10), and that the high priests of Israel “serve unto the example and shadow of heavenly things” (Hebrews 8:5)."),
("p", "The prophets had said much the same from the other side. When Isaiah asks, “To what purpose is the multitude of your sacrifices unto me?” (Isaiah 1:11), and Psalm 51:16 says, “thou delightest not in burnt offering,” the prophets are saying that the offerings do not do the thing the worshipers hope they do. Hebrews 10:4 says it in a sentence: the blood of bulls and goats cannot take away sins. The two statements agree, and they are made for different reasons. The prophets say it to people who are using the offering to cover their sins. The letter says it to people who are asking what the offerings were for."),
("sub", "8.3 The quotation of Psalm 40"),
("p", "The letter then quotes the psalm. “Wherefore when he cometh into the world, he saith, Sacrifice and offering thou wouldest not, but a body hast thou prepared me: in burnt offerings and sacrifices for sin thou hast had no pleasure. Then said I, Lo, I come (in the volume of the book it is written of me,) to do thy will, O God” (Hebrews 10:5–7). Psalm 40:6–8, as chapter 1 read it, says: “Sacrifice and offering thou didst not desire; mine ears hast thou opened: burnt offering and sin offering hast thou not required. Then said I, Lo, I come: in the volume of the book it is written of me, I delight to do thy will, O my God.”"),
("p", "Two things differ in the English. Where the psalm has “mine ears hast thou opened,” the letter has “a body hast thou prepared me.” And the psalm’s “burnt offering and sin offering hast thou not required” stands in the letter as “in burnt offerings and sacrifices for sin thou hast had no pleasure.” The volume reports both wordings as they stand in the King James Version and builds no theory on the difference. The speaker is the same in both: the one who says, Lo, I come, and says that it is written of him in the volume of the book. The letter introduces the words with “when he cometh into the world, he saith,” and so reads the psalm as the speech of the one who comes. The volume reports that reading."),
("p", "What the quotation shares with the psalm is the sentence of rank. The psalm sets the offerings against the will done, and so does the letter. In the letter’s next verses the four named offerings are those of the psalm: “Above when he said, Sacrifice and offering and burnt offerings and offering for sin thou wouldest not, neither hadst pleasure therein; which are offered by the law” (Hebrews 10:8). The phrase “which are offered by the law” is the letter’s gloss on the four. It tells the reader that the offerings the psalm names are the Law’s offerings, and that the “not” of the psalm applies to them."),
("sub", "8.4 The first and the second"),
("p", "Verse 9 is the verse that causes the difficulty. “Then said he, Lo, I come to do thy will, O God. He taketh away the first, that he may establish the second.” The first is the set of offerings that the psalm said the LORD did not desire. The second is the will done. The writer reads the psalm as announcing that what the LORD wanted was never the offerings in themselves and that the one who comes does the will. Verse 10 then says what the will accomplished: “By the which will we are sanctified through the offering of the body of Jesus Christ once for all.”"),
("p", "The volume reads the verse as follows. The letter says that Christ’s offering does what the offerings of the Law could not do: it takes sins away. In that sense he takes away the first, as the means by which sin is dealt with, and establishes the second, the will done and the body offered once for all. The verse does not say that the prophets set the offerings aside. It says that what the psalm already said, that the offerings were not the thing the LORD desired and required, comes to its point in the one who says, Lo, I come. The prophets’ sentences are not abolished by the letter, and they are not made false. They are brought to the one they pointed toward."),
("p", "The verses that follow say the same thing in the letter’s own terms. “And every priest standeth daily ministering and offering oftentimes the same sacrifices, which can never take away sins: but this man, after he had offered one sacrifice for sins for ever, sat down on the right hand of God” (Hebrews 10:11, 12). “For by one offering he hath perfected for ever them that are sanctified” (Hebrews 10:14). The letter calls the Holy Ghost a witness to this, and it quotes the covenant of Jeremiah 31: “This is the covenant that I will make with them after those days, saith the Lord, I will put my laws into their hearts, and in their minds will I write them; and their sins and iniquities will I remember no more” (Hebrews 10:15–17). Verse 18 ends the argument: “Now where remission of these is, there is no more offering for sin.” Study 24 reads that finality in detail, and the volume cites it."),
("sub", "8.5 What the letter does not say"),
("p", "It does not say that the prophets condemned the Law’s sacrifices as sinful. The prophets’ sentences were about how the worshipers used them, and the letter’s are about what they could do. It does not say that burnt offerings will never be offered again. Hebrews 10:18 speaks of offering for sin, and the sentence is about remission of sins. Typology Commentary Volume 1 reads Ezekiel 40–48 as a Kingdom temple with memorial offerings, and nothing in this volume argues against that reading: offerings that never took away sins can be offered as a remembrance without taking them away. The volume does not argue the point, and cites the Typology volume."),
("p", "Nor does the letter say anything of the Body of Christ in these verses. It speaks of those whom the covenant of Jeremiah 31 concerns, and it holds the Body at a distance in the standing sentence given above. The blood of Christ is the ground, and the Body remembers it. The Body is not the party to the covenant the letter quotes. The prophets’ charges are in this volume read as charges brought against Israel’s worship; the letter’s verdict is read as a verdict inside Israel’s line, and nothing in either is made a charge against, or a rule for, the worship of the Body."),
("ctbl", "Psalm 40 and Hebrews 10 side by side.", [
    ("Point", "Psalm 40:6–8", "Hebrews 10:5–10"),
    ("The offerings named", "Sacrifice, offering, burnt offering, sin offering", "Sacrifice, offering, burnt offerings, sacrifices for sin; “which are offered by the law”"),
    ("What is said of them", "“Thou didst not desire … hast thou not required”", "“Thou wouldest not … thou hast had no pleasure”"),
    ("The body and the ears", "“Mine ears hast thou opened”", "“A body hast thou prepared me”"),
    ("The speaker", "“Then said I, Lo, I come”", "“When he cometh into the world, he saith”"),
    ("What is set against the offerings", "“I delight to do thy will, O my God: yea, thy law is within my heart”", "“to do thy will, O God”; “He taketh away the first, that he may establish the second”"),
  ], [0.22, 0.38, 0.4]),
("sub", "8.6 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Hebrews 10:1–4 says what the Law’s sacrifices could not do: they were a shadow, repeated year by year, and could not take away sins.",
    "The letter quotes Psalm 40 and says the offerings named are those “offered by the law”; the volume reports the two wordings of the clause on the body and the ears without a theory.",
    "Hebrews 10:9 is read as the letter’s argument that the will done takes the place of the offerings as the means of dealing with sin; it does not say the prophets abolished the offerings.",
    "The letter and the prophets agree that the offerings were never the thing that took sins away, and they say it to different people for different reasons.",
    "The letter is read inside Israel’s line; nothing in it is made a charge against, or a rule for, the Body, and Ezekiel 40–48 is left to Typology Commentary Volume 1.",
  ]),
]
